1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:05,879
From appearing in Simpsons musicals, Stellum, Megatron, Eric Cartman, Donkey Kong,

2
00:00:06,379 --> 00:00:10,820
and somehow the Powerpuff Girls, Blossom, are you okay?

3
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:12,339
Uh, you're trippy.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:16,960
Seriously. Turning Blossom into a Cartman is what I'd call a crime against humanity.

5
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:23,500
But seriously, South Park has become such a legendary show that almost everyone else has tried to feature them in their cartoons.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:29,239
So today, I'll be going through all the animated shows where a visual reference of South Park is clearly featured,

7
00:00:29,679 --> 00:00:31,160
starting with The Simpsons.

8
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:35,979
In The Bart of War, the episode literally starts with Bart Milhouse checking out an episode of South Park.

9
00:00:36,359 --> 00:00:39,200
Next on Comedy Central, an all-new South Park.

10
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:42,340
I hear those kids' voices are done by grown-ups.

11
00:00:42,759 --> 00:00:44,079
Hey, there's nothing wrong with that.

12
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:50,359
I can't believe we paid to see a band with Steve Guttenberg, Farty the Crippled Robot, and Calista Flockhart.

13
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:52,840
Look who's in my fart, OJ.

14
00:00:53,619 --> 00:00:54,539
I'm gonna kill you all.

15
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,560
Now I'm gonna find the real killer.

16
00:00:58,679 --> 00:01:00,820
Yeah, that looks like an episode of South Park, all right.

17
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:02,659
Just drawn way worse somehow.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,040
There isn't really much of a point to this cameo,

19
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:08,340
except that Marge tells the boys they shouldn't watch something that isn't life-affirming.

20
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:09,900
Uh, roasted, I guess?

21
00:01:10,159 --> 00:01:12,060
Then there's the episode Oh, Brother, Where Bart Thou?

22
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:13,500
where Bart decides he wants a baby brother.

23
00:01:13,819 --> 00:01:16,359
However, convincing his father proves to be a pointless exercise.

24
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:17,900
I want a baby brother.

25
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Lisa got a sister.

26
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:21,239
Why can't you have another boy?

27
00:01:21,659 --> 00:01:26,579
I love you kids, but I'm only going to the hospital one more time in my life and I ain't coming out.

28
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:27,680
Girls are easy.

29
00:01:28,099 --> 00:01:30,219
Girls make birthday cards with glitter on them.

30
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,400
Girls love daddy.

31
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,780
Girls can marry a hockey player and get me seats to hockey games.

32
00:01:34,879 --> 00:01:37,480
Yes. All the important things in life.

33
00:01:37,859 --> 00:01:40,840
The South Park reference soon follows as Bart, Milhouse, Nelson,

34
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:45,079
and Ralph play out the classic South Park bus stop scene while discussing Bart's dilemma.

35
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,900
My dad is such a jerk.

36
00:01:48,159 --> 00:01:49,980
I want a baby brother and he said no.

37
00:01:50,159 --> 00:01:52,400
The way my mom nearly tricked Charles Barkley.

38
00:01:52,719 --> 00:01:54,980
Maybe you could trick your parents into making a baby.

39
00:01:55,379 --> 00:01:55,739
Trick them.

40
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:56,760
I like it.

41
00:01:57,060 --> 00:01:57,459
But how?

42
00:01:57,659 --> 00:01:58,599
A romantic dinner?

43
00:01:59,099 --> 00:02:00,239
That's a great idea.

44
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:05,640
Weird. I could have sworn Kenny Ralph said I love glue.

45
00:02:06,799 --> 00:02:08,240
Oh my god, I killed Kenny.

46
00:02:08,379 --> 00:02:10,340
Ralph. No, I killed Kenny yesterday.

47
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:13,840
And I was just about to say Bart should just adopt a little Kenny Ralph as his baby brother,

48
00:02:13,979 --> 00:02:15,439
but I guess that's not going to happen.

49
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:19,919
On to gorgeous Grandpa, where we learned that Grandpa used to be a semi-famous wrestler who wore wigs and

50
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,000
went by the fabulous name of Glamorous Gone Free.

51
00:02:23,199 --> 00:02:24,539
Oh, and it turns out Mr.

52
00:02:24,719 --> 00:02:26,219
Burns of all people is his biggest fan.

53
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:31,560
Please Abe, let me buy you dinner in exchange for the tales of the golden age of professional grappling.

54
00:02:31,979 --> 00:02:34,120
But Grandpa, I want to hear about old time wrestling too.

55
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:37,199
I've been trying to forget those days for 50 years.

56
00:02:37,419 --> 00:02:39,050
Well, it looks like you've made a new fan.

57
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:40,979
It'd be a shame to let him down.

58
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:47,120
The emotional blackmail works and the three of them go on a fancy dinner where Burns tries to convince Grandpa to come out of retirement.

59
00:02:47,419 --> 00:02:52,539
Grandpa was hated for being pretty dang good at wrestling and he doesn't want to go back to being the bad guy again.

60
00:02:52,919 --> 00:02:58,520
Which is a concept so difficult for Burns to fathom that it makes him burst out into song and sing about famous pop culture villains.

61
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:02,080
Stellan, Megatron, Eric Cartman, Donkey Kong.

62
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:07,139
Yeah, of course Eric Cartman would be included in a list of villains Burns deems admirable.

63
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:10,319
He is, after all, one of the most evil cartoon villains of all time.

64
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:14,680
The following South Park cameo happens in the couch gag in The Cad in the Hat,

65
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,819
when Homer notices something's off in their living room.

66
00:03:19,479 --> 00:03:20,069
Where's our sailboat?

67
00:03:20,639 --> 00:03:21,699
We really tied the room together.

68
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:25,539
Maybe another animated show stole it.

69
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,379
That painting was great.

70
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:29,439
Derivative amateur seascape.

71
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:35,560
Homer seems to immediately suspect who the thieving show is as he rushes out of the Simpsons set and straight into another.

72
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:39,740
Possible. Is this a TV show or did I walk into a third grade shoebox diorama?

73
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:41,300
Did you steal my painting?

74
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:42,120
No, I got it.

75
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:44,560
Yeah! He he he he.

76
00:03:45,219 --> 00:03:46,740
Out of the mouths of babes.

77
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,740
Yep, there's the South Park Core 4.

78
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:50,300
Or at least a rip off of them.

79
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:52,620
Just look at their cute tiny little orange noses.

80
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:56,000
Homer eventually finds the painting and we see the Core 4 one more time.

81
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:02,379
Oh my days, he killed all of them.

82
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:03,819
Homer, you frick.

83
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:06,599
Then there's the opening theme of The Squirt and the Whale,

84
00:04:06,639 --> 00:04:10,060
where we see Bart's blackboard scribble speaking directly to the South Park fam.

85
00:04:13,259 --> 00:04:19,399
That message was in reference to threats that Trey Parker and Matt Stone received after featuring the Prophet Muhammad in the episode 201.

86
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:21,660
Come on, the door's open.

87
00:04:21,819 --> 00:04:22,959
Dr. Mephesto, we need your help.

88
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,060
Doctor, this is...

89
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:28,779
We want you to clone him so we can give his copies to Gingers and Barbara Streisand.

90
00:04:28,959 --> 00:04:34,279
As you can see, both the figure and name of the Prophet ended up being heavily censored following the threats of the show creators received.

91
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:39,300
Even The Simpsons ended up changing their blackboard shoutout because the same episode's intro now looks like this.

92
00:04:42,879 --> 00:04:44,839
If you don't know, that says I don't know French,

93
00:04:44,839 --> 00:04:49,850
which honestly could still be a reference to the South Park episode since all those bleeps made it sound like absolute gibberish.

94
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:55,000
The final easter egg happens in the last segment of Treehouse of Horror 33, titled Simpsons World.

95
00:04:55,199 --> 00:04:56,290
It's a parody of Westworld,

96
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:01,180
only it's all Simpsons lore and the Simpsons family are those freakishly lifelike robots who've become self-aware.

97
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:04,480
Okay, act like robots.

98
00:05:04,949 --> 00:05:06,339
Don't have a cow, man.

99
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:07,939
Catchphrases, everyone.

100
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:09,040
Dinner's ready?

101
00:05:09,319 --> 00:05:14,139
The four sentient Simpsons manage to escape the amusement park in the end, only to find themselves in another one.

102
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:15,430
Oh, you got big eyes.

103
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:16,579
Turn off the headlights.

104
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:17,540
Forget I said anything.

105
00:05:17,839 --> 00:05:18,300
Take your time.

106
00:05:18,699 --> 00:05:19,250
Sweaty cheese.

107
00:05:19,759 --> 00:05:25,759
Yep, that's Bob's Burgers, only it's also set in the robot-filled amusement park and there are other amusement parks as well.

108
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:33,060
There's South Park Park in the shape of Stan's little hat.

109
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:34,389
Listen, I don't know about you,

110
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:38,259
but I personally would love to see the carnage the sentient Cartman would unleash in a place like that.

111
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:41,339
Right, now let's look at the time Rick and Morty featured South Park.

112
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:42,139
Sort of.

113
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:45,240
The visual gag involves the famous South Park 4 clothing,

114
00:05:45,399 --> 00:05:50,420
which a lot of shows use because it's so iconic and easily recognizable, only in the Vat of Acid episode,

115
00:05:50,579 --> 00:05:53,279
it's been slightly changed in color for some reason.

116
00:05:53,459 --> 00:05:58,139
The easter egg pops up after Morty asks Rick to invent a save-your-game device that can be used in real life.

117
00:05:58,279 --> 00:06:01,019
Naturally, Morty uses this new gadget very responsibly.

118
00:06:01,399 --> 00:06:02,600
Who wants to do some math?

119
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:03,240
I'll do it.

120
00:06:03,279 --> 00:06:04,579
A bold move, Morty Smith.

121
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:06,259
Oh, I've got something to deliver.

122
00:06:14,939 --> 00:06:17,160
A device like that should never exist.

123
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:21,639
Morty obviously goes ahead and uses it on practically everything until he meets a special lady.

124
00:06:29,410 --> 00:06:31,879
The two hit it off and are very much in love,

125
00:06:31,899 --> 00:06:36,389
and we're treated to an Up-style montage of their blossoming and at times challenging relationship.

126
00:06:46,350 --> 00:06:48,920
Morty at least realizes he needs to do something to save the relationship,

127
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:54,100
so he surprises the new girlfriend with tickets to some location that is not an alien-infested interdimensional hellhole,

128
00:06:54,199 --> 00:06:56,560
and off they go to enjoy some away time in Ohno.

129
00:07:04,449 --> 00:07:08,019
And they went from traveling lovers to plane crash survivors real quick,

130
00:07:08,199 --> 00:07:12,560
and it is here in the snowy mountain at the crash site that we get the cheeky South Park reference.

131
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:24,800
Okay, so rotisserie shoe for dinner and cannibalism aside,

132
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:29,360
these four survivors are obviously a nod to the South Park 4, only with a few adjustments.

133
00:07:29,779 --> 00:07:34,500
One guy's wearing Stan's hat, the unknown woman's wearing an Ushanka just like Kyle, only it's not green,

134
00:07:34,639 --> 00:07:35,660
but her jacket is.

135
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:39,240
Morty's just like Kenny, so I guess that makes his girlfriend Cartman.

136
00:07:39,779 --> 00:07:42,899
Which could very well be the joke here because she's like the opposite of Cartman,

137
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,480
but Morty on the other hand ends up resembling Kenny in more ways than one in this episode.

138
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,759
You weren't saving your place and going back.

139
00:07:49,139 --> 00:07:50,060
Respect time travel.

140
00:07:50,199 --> 00:07:53,420
Do-over as it was isolating a moment in time, splitting your probable self,

141
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:59,480
and shunting you into a near-duplicate, equally probable reality, transporting you into it at the moment of parallel determination.

142
00:07:59,779 --> 00:08:01,720
Pretty nifty, time crystals are a bitch and a half.

143
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:05,399
The only real hitch is that there was already a U in each probable dimension,

144
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:06,939
so we had to solve for that.

145
00:08:09,329 --> 00:08:14,160
What this basically means, as Rick then explains, is that Morty prestiged himself.

146
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:16,939
Every time he hit that supposed do-over button,

147
00:08:17,279 --> 00:08:20,779
he killed a version of himself to prevent thousands of Morty duplicates from running around.

148
00:08:21,019 --> 00:08:21,420
So, you know.

149
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:23,360
Oh my god, they killed Morty!

150
00:08:23,459 --> 00:08:24,399
Again, again, and again.

151
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:27,160
Okay, let's look at the time South Park showed up in Family Guy.

152
00:08:27,339 --> 00:08:29,779
The first visual reference can be found in an Emmy-winning episode,

153
00:08:29,899 --> 00:08:33,240
the one where Peter decides that the show isn't doing enough to bag the Coveted TV Award.

154
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:39,980
Kids, it's me, tu madre.

155
00:08:40,470 --> 00:08:41,820
Lois gr- Aww, yeah.

156
00:08:42,139 --> 00:08:42,879
Come on in, Lois.

157
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:43,539
My wife, Lois.

158
00:08:43,779 --> 00:08:44,259
I'll get my wife.

159
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:47,980
Yeah, so his idea of how to win an Emmy is to recast his wife and make some sexist jokes.

160
00:08:48,399 --> 00:08:49,740
Oh, and also this, apparently.

161
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:52,980
I am a woman, and always have been.

162
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:54,100
Ay, Dios mio!

163
00:08:54,519 --> 00:08:58,919
I've made plans to undergo surgery so my wang don't get in the way of the woman what I am.

164
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:02,919
Lois, I'll still be a parent to our children, but now I'll be a trans parent.

165
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:06,860
Excuse me while I fix my eyes from rolling all the way to the back of my skull.

166
00:09:07,419 --> 00:09:10,940
The ploy obviously doesn't work, so Peter tries to go for best drama, best documentary,

167
00:09:11,259 --> 00:09:12,639
and, uh, even reality TV.

168
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:14,440
He eventually ends up on The Daily Show,

169
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:18,080
where Bill Maher tells him that there's a conspiracy preventing Family Guy from ever winning an Emmy.

170
00:09:18,259 --> 00:09:21,600
Peter decides to visit the local library and do some investigation, and we get to see this.

171
00:09:28,559 --> 00:09:31,600
There's South Park and the times they managed to win an Emmy.

172
00:09:31,779 --> 00:09:35,580
Peter deduces that there's a secret society making sure the same shows win year after year,

173
00:09:35,659 --> 00:09:37,960
because I guess that's just the kind of logic you can expect from Peter Griffin.

174
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:42,360
The next South Park cameo is way funnier and can be found in the intro of V is for Mystery,

175
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:45,039
where we see Stewie and Brian roleplay as Sherlock Holmes and Dr.

176
00:09:45,139 --> 00:09:57,120
Watson. Aw, yes, the classic Sherlock Holmes in the case of the ever-dying fourth grader.

177
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:58,919
Right on over to American Dad we go,

178
00:09:59,139 --> 00:10:02,840
where two characters randomly find themselves in the South Park universe in a blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment.

179
00:10:03,039 --> 00:10:05,879
With this reference, I'm showing you the episode The Longest Distance Relationship,

180
00:10:06,139 --> 00:10:09,980
where it's been a year since Jeff was abducted by a UFO after learning that Roger is an alien.

181
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:14,720
No! Harry!

182
00:10:15,519 --> 00:10:18,740
No! Roger!

183
00:10:19,039 --> 00:10:20,039
Make them turn around!

184
00:10:20,799 --> 00:10:25,240
Super harsh, my dude, but it finally looks like Jeff has found a way to get back home and back to Hayley.

185
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:26,120
Found a way home?

186
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:29,200
Our ship found a wormhole that will take us straight to Earth!

187
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:32,019
We can finally get back to living our lives together?

188
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:33,840
Yeah, babe, I'll be home soon!

189
00:10:34,159 --> 00:10:37,840
So they just have to travel through a wormhole and hope their insides don't become their outsides?

190
00:10:38,179 --> 00:10:39,100
Aces, let's watch.

191
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:42,799
Wow, wormhole!

192
00:10:43,059 --> 00:10:45,639
Like, staring into the face of God!

193
00:10:45,879 --> 00:10:47,379
It's so beautiful!

194
00:10:47,659 --> 00:10:48,460
It's unbelievable!

195
00:10:49,559 --> 00:10:51,200
It's pretty unbelievable, alright.

196
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:55,509
And there, as you can see, is Jeff and Sinbad, as they would look in the world of South Park.

197
00:10:55,820 --> 00:11:00,299
It's definitely better than being an alien with six eyes or whatever those two pink thingies are supposed to be.

198
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:05,200
Moving on to the show about a blue cat who lives in an amazing world that can also be kind of terrifying sometimes, it's Gumball!

199
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:09,120
In the episode The Potato, Darwin questions his eating habits thanks to his friend Idaho,

200
00:11:09,320 --> 00:11:12,759
who is a tiny potato and has to witness things like this on a semi-regular basis.

201
00:11:18,919 --> 00:11:20,019
Mmm, fluffy!

202
00:11:20,759 --> 00:11:23,080
Yes, Darwin is Idaho's Hannibal Lecter.

203
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,759
Also, a fish eating potatoes?

204
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:25,399
That ain't right.

205
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:29,820
Anyway, Gumball explains to Darwin how his actions affect their could-be-tater-tot friend,

206
00:11:29,919 --> 00:11:33,820
and Darwin decides to swear off eating any potatoes in any form forever, which, you know,

207
00:11:33,899 --> 00:11:35,440
is so much easier said than done.

208
00:11:35,840 --> 00:11:37,480
Okay, dude, is there anywhere else we've missed?

209
00:11:37,620 --> 00:11:39,039
We've cleared out your locker and your desk.

210
00:11:39,279 --> 00:11:41,019
Is there anywhere else?

211
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:42,340
I don't recall.

212
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:46,820
I think we've got the- Thanks for leading me to your secret supply!

213
00:11:47,039 --> 00:11:47,679
Stay strong, bro.

214
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:50,519
This is a very emotional time for me.

215
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:51,620
But it's fine!

216
00:11:52,179 --> 00:11:53,220
Everything is great!

217
00:11:53,519 --> 00:11:55,779
I keep getting these horrible, devastating moveswings!

218
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:58,100
Yep, that is the cold turkey effect right there.

219
00:11:58,279 --> 00:11:59,980
Gumball tries to help his pal get over his cravings,

220
00:12:00,379 --> 00:12:02,440
but nothing works, and Darwin eventually caves.

221
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:03,419
That's it!

222
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:06,740
No! I'm sorry, but this is who I am!

223
00:12:08,250 --> 00:12:09,240
Huh. Do I look okay?

224
00:12:09,519 --> 00:12:10,360
I don't feel any different.

225
00:12:10,639 --> 00:12:11,360
Potato. Potato?

226
00:12:11,580 --> 00:12:12,179
Potato, potato.

227
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:12,460
Potato, potato.

228
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:17,019
Yes, all his friends have turned into potato heads who totally look like every Canadian character in South Park,

229
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:17,700
only creepy, here.

230
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:20,259
Potato! Potato!

231
00:12:20,259 --> 00:12:21,100
Hello, Darwin!

232
00:12:21,279 --> 00:12:22,100
